---
/**
 * RiskLadder — the signature diagram (spec §6).
 *
 * A project timeline showing where owner risk is actually created
 * (contract execution, buyout, first draws, change order accumulation)
 * against where owners typically start paying attention (mid-construction,
 * when correction is expensive). Makes the argument for early engagement.
 *
 * Pure inline SVG, no JS, full text alternative for screen readers.
 */
---

<figure class="my-10" aria-labelledby="riskladder-title" role="group">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 760 380"
    class="w-full"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="riskladder-title riskladder-desc"
    font-family="'JetBrains Mono Variable', ui-monospace, monospace"
  >
    <title id="riskladder-title">Where owner risk is created versus where owners start paying attention</title>
    <desc id="riskladder-desc">
      A project timeline from design through closeout. Most owner risk is locked in early — at contract
      execution, buyout, and the first draws — while most owners only begin paying close attention
      mid-construction, after the leverage to correct course cheaply is gone.
    </desc>

    <!-- Timeline axis -->
    <line x1="40" y1="300" x2="720" y2="300" stroke="#16181A" stroke-width="1.5" />

    <!-- Phase ticks and labels -->
    <g fill="#6E6E6E" font-size="11">
      <line x1="40" y1="295" x2="40" y2="305" stroke="#16181A" />
      <text x="40" y="322">Design</text>
      <line x1="180" y1="295" x2="180" y2="305" stroke="#16181A" />
      <text x="180" y="322">Contract</text>
      <line x1="320" y1="295" x2="320" y2="305" stroke="#16181A" />
      <text x="320" y="322">Buyout / first draws</text>
      <line x1="500" y1="295" x2="500" y2="305" stroke="#16181A" />
      <text x="500" y="322">Mid-construction</text>
      <line x1="650" y1="295" x2="650" y2="305" stroke="#16181A" />
      <text x="650" y="322">Closeout</text>
    </g>

    <!-- Risk-created curve: high early, declining -->
    <path
      d="M 40 90 C 180 85, 300 120, 420 210 C 520 275, 620 285, 720 290"
      fill="none"
      stroke="#1F4E46"
      stroke-width="2.5"
    />
    <g fill="#1F4E46" font-size="12" font-weight="600">
      <text x="48" y="48">Where risk is created</text>
    </g>

    <!-- Attention curve: low early, rising mid-construction -->
    <path
      d="M 40 285 C 200 283, 360 275, 460 220 C 540 175, 600 130, 700 110"
      fill="none"
      stroke="#7A2E22"
      stroke-width="2.5"
      stroke-dasharray="6 4"
    />
    <g fill="#7A2E22" font-size="12" font-weight="600">
      <text x="530" y="105">Where attention usually starts</text>
    </g>

    <!-- Risk-creation event markers -->
    <g fill="#16181A" font-size="10.5">
      <circle cx="180" cy="86" r="4" fill="#1F4E46" />
      <text x="60" y="118">Contract executed:</text>
      <text x="60" y="131" fill="#6E6E6E">terms fixed</text>

      <circle cx="320" cy="126" r="4" fill="#1F4E46" />
      <text x="335" y="112">Buyout: margins set,</text>
      <text x="335" y="125" fill="#6E6E6E">allowances committed</text>

      <circle cx="420" cy="210" r="4" fill="#1F4E46" />
      <line x1="428" y1="208" x2="552" y2="204" stroke="#CFCAC2" stroke-width="1" />
      <text x="558" y="208">Change orders accumulate</text>
    </g>

    <!-- The gap annotation -->
    <g>
      <line x1="245" y1="102" x2="245" y2="278" stroke="#CFCAC2" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="2 3" />
      <text x="255" y="196" fill="#6E6E6E" font-size="10.5">the gap an owner&#8217;s</text>
      <text x="255" y="209" fill="#6E6E6E" font-size="10.5">representative closes</text>
    </g>

    <!-- Y axis label -->
    <text x="24" y="200" fill="#6E6E6E" font-size="10.5" transform="rotate(-90 24 200)" text-anchor="middle">
      exposure / attention
    </text>
  </svg>

  <figcaption class="annotation mt-3 max-w-prose">
    Fig. 01 — Most of an owner&rsquo;s exposure is fixed before ground breaks: at contract execution, at buyout,
    and in the first draws. Most owners begin paying close attention mid-construction, after the inexpensive
    corrections are gone. Representation belongs at the left side of this chart.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
